My first post here. hi everyone.

Having read the rest of the thread, I've dreamed up a variation on
this idea: How about an amulet that saves the players life by
transferring them into the body of a nearby monster?

I can think of two implementations for this:

1> When the character dies (and they are wearing the amulet), the game
draws up a shortlist of the 5 or 6 nearest monsters, then randomly
picks one. Your character's memories and mind (ie player control) are
transferred into the unfortunate monster.

To prevent exploitation of this item (ie using it as a polymorph by
clearing the level of all but one powerful hostile monster/pet and
then committing suicide) maybe a few lichens, fungi and sewer rats
should be spawned elsewhere on the level at time of death on levels
where there aren't many monsters.

2> When the character dies (and they are wearing the amulet) then a
ghost appears above the corpse. This ghost is under player control and
can pass through walls like a normal ghost, but is weak, slow, can't
pick anything up and is vulnerable to attacks from monsters. Those
attacks can destroy the ghost, resulting in the end of the game.
The ghost-player has a hundred or so turns to find a suitable host
body to possess. To possess a monster, the ghost merely has to move
into the same square as it. The monster gets a chance to resist (based
on magical resistance and/or intelligence), a successful resist
results in the ghost simply being thrown of the square, so it can try
again with that or another monster. Obviously certain monsters should
be immune from possession (WoY for example). Once a ghost posesses a
body, it cannot leave it.

In both cases, the new body retains all of its stats, intrinsics and
so on, but the player can then loot his old body as previously
discussed, although much of equipment may be cursed like a corpse
found in a bones file.
If you end up in an "enemy" monster's body, any pets you used to have
will have to be killed, avoided or re-tamed. If you voluntarily take
over a pet's body, that should incur the wrath of the Gods as though
you'd killed it.

Both implementations open up some interesting possibilities, anyway.
Not least a new way of plundering a shop- steal the shopkeeper's
body=-)

